# gvsoc Snitch model fix: FP instructions with an integer source/destination register must take the FPU
# sequencer's bypass lane ('nseq' tag) like their double-precision counterparts already do. Without it
# fcvt.w.s / flt.s / feq.s / fmv.x.s / fclass.s (and the fp16/fp8 scalar variants) are queued in the FPU
# sequence buffer while the integer core runs ahead: the core reads the destination register before the
# subsystem has written it, and the late write-back clobbers newer integer values (WAW). Whether it bites
# depends on the fill level of the sequence buffer, i.e. on cycle-level timing / code layout. Symptom in
# softhier-mlir: sh_softmax_rows computed softmax(-x) for some binaries (the 2^k factor of exp() was built
# from a clobbered a5), so `siglip --cluster all` failed while trivial code changes made it pass.
# Apply in /app/install/softhier/core/models/cpu/iss/isa_gen (and the copy in install/generators/cpu/iss/isa_gen),
# regenerate build/core/isa_snitch_rv32imfdva.cpp (gapy build / the ISA generator) and rebuild + reinstall
# gen_isa_snitch_rv32imfdva_cpp_60335333_{optim,debug} (the integer core: the sequencer decides on its tags).
# Found 2026-10-08 (softhier-mlir, branch agent/mesh-attn).
